    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i'm looking at some paperwork for a house i'm buying in seattle and it keeps mentioning \"escrow.\" i don't really get what that actually means or what the person in charge of it actually does. like why can't i just pay the seller directly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow is a neutral third party that holds funds and documents during your home purchase to ensure both you and the seller are protected. That person or company acts as an impartial neutral, releasing money only when all conditions of the contract are met, rather than letting one side pay directly while the other delays closing. While you could theoretically bypass escrow to save fees, doing so involves significant legal risks if the seller later discovers mortgage liens or title issues that you won't be able to fix afterward.",
